// The router's own two prompts (epic memql#5137, D7 and D9).
//
// BOTH ARE STRUCTURED-OUTPUT AND BOTH ARE CHEAP ON PURPOSE. One turns a
// person's sentence into a rule they then confirm; the other classifies a
// free-form request into a level. Neither writes anything, and neither is on a
// hot path: the compiler runs once per rule an owner authors, and the
// classifier runs only when the deterministic table in component/router has no
// opinion.
//
// `compileRule` IS THE ONE PROMPT WHOSE SUBJECT IS WHERE WORK MAY RUN, which
// is why the shipped locked rule `compilerLocalOnly` pins it to this cluster's
// own hardware at precedence 120. Sending it to a vendor would mean the act
// that decides what leaves the cluster is itself the thing that leaves, before
// the rule exists to forbid it. The rule matches on the prompt NAME, so this
// name is a contract: `router.CompilerPromptName` in Go must agree with it, and
// component/router's own test asserts that both are the tree's.

/// Compile one sentence into a routing rule the person then confirms.
///
/// Structured output: returns {name, when, level, policy, precedence,
/// onUnavailable, excludes, restatement}. `restatement` is the compiler's OWN
/// account of what it produced, in its own words -- never the sentence handed
/// back, which would prove nothing about what it understood. The person reads
/// the restatement beside their sentence and the simulation beside both.
///
/// It may NAME a policy and may not mint one: a chain minted from a sentence
/// would be a spending decision in a place no review looks.
@level("strong")
@templateFile("prompts/compileRule.tmpl")
prompt compileRule {
  sentence     string!   @description("What the person typed, verbatim.")
  whenKeys     []string! @description("The closed @when vocabulary this cluster's loader accepts. The compiler may use no other condition key; one outside this list produces a rule that refuses to load.")
  policies     []object! @description("The policies this cluster has registered, as {name, description, chain}. The compiler picks one by name.")
  fleetModels  []object  @description("Optional: the models this fleet currently serves, as {modelId, category}, so an exclusion can name a real one. Absent on a cluster with no machines paired.")
  levels       []string! @description("The closed level vocabulary: fast, strong, reasoning, embeddings.")
}

/// Classify a free-form request into the level it needs.
///
/// Structured output: returns {level, confidence, reason}. It is reached ONLY
/// when component/router's deterministic table had no opinion -- and the table
/// answers first for a reason: a classifier in front of every call is the cost
/// this whole program exists to avoid, so the cheap path must stay the common
/// one.
///
/// FACTS BEFORE GUESSES. The table decides on what the request STATES (a
/// declared level, an embeddings modality, a known prompt name); this prompt
/// only ever sees what none of those settled.
@level("fast")
@templateFile("prompts/classifyRequest.tmpl")
prompt classifyRequest {
  request      string!   @description("The free-form request to classify.")
  levels       []string! @description("The closed level vocabulary, with what each one is for.")
  ruledOut     []string  @description("Optional: levels the deterministic table has already excluded, with why. The classifier must not choose one of these.")
}

/// Compose a routing-policy draft for a person to review. Does not change routing.
@level("strong")
@templateFile("prompts/composeRoutingPolicy.tmpl")
prompt composeRoutingPolicy {
  sentence string!
  catalog string!
}
